Z||000003||88formula||11-14-2000||05:02 PM||||There are the traditional modifications like camshaft, ported heads and headers. The 3.4-liter engine responds very well to these modifications. I am using the GM 12363220 performance ($200 with lifters) camshaft that GM uses in the 3.4-liter crate engine. You can get ported heads at Norris Racing ($950) <A HREF="http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.norrisracingtech.com/home.html</A>  . You can get a great set of headers from FOCOA ($600), which are the one's I want to buy <A HREF="http://www.fieroowners.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroowners.com</A>  . You will have to call them about the headers. Those modifications should get you about 202 horsepower @ 5500 rpm and 230 ft. lbs. of torque @ 4000 rpm. The stock 3.4-liter engine is good for 165 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 208 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. You can also put in higher compression pistons ($230) (9.7) and blueprint the block. I am doing this also. The pistons from a Twin cam 3.4-liter engine will fit in the OHV engine and give you 9.7 compression.<P>You may want to upgrade to a performance coil like the MSD #8226 coil from the Jegs catalog ($38) for better high end spark and have a custom prom chip done to get the most from your modifications. <P>I forgot to say an aluminum flywheel is a great modification. It gives you more horsepower with no loose in anything else. You can get them from George Miller (fieroshop). <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-14-2000||11:11 PM||||88Formula,<BR>Isn't the 3.4 crate-motor cam very similar to the Camaro cam and the stock Fiero cam? I was under the impression that they were all performance cams, with only minor (if any) differences. <P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||11-15-2000||06:24 PM||||The difference in performance is similar with the exception of the GM performance camshaft making more top end power than the stock camshaft. The GM performance camshaft is only 49 State legal so that should tell you right there that the stock camshaft is not the same thing as the Gm performance 12363220 camshaft.  <P>The specifications are as follows measured @ .050".<BR>Stock camshaft<BR>Duration 195/214 lift .390/. 405 This camshaft makes 165 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 208 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm.<P>GM performance 12363220<BR>Duration 204/216 lift .427/. 454 This camshaft makes 169 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 210 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000-3500 rpm.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||mshill||11-15-2000||06:32 PM||||Good info.  Given the moderately small increases I will wait until I have to rebuild the 3.4 to put it in.  My 3.4L has 49K on it now so I will be driving 100K before I will be doing this mod.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bill H||11-30-2000||10:59 PM|||| Does anyone know where to get a custom prom from? And do all 3.4s need to have the starter moved? I am looking into the crate 3.4 from GM performance parts.  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000008||mshill||12-01-2000||12:24 AM||||Bill,  the GM crate 3.4L engine is primarily for the S10 pickups that initially had a 2.8 in them.  They are setup for RWD so, yes the starter needs to be moved on those as well.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||N_Man||12-01-2000||01:02 AM||||Do a search for the 3.4 that fierosound has built.  Every part number is there, plus every practical mod has been done.||||reg||1
